in the transcriptomics one course we  cover key concepts needed to understand  the analysis of NGS gene expression data  we've already discussed how genes are  expressed in the cell and how we can run  a bioinformatics analysis pipeline to  process RNA seek data which generates a  table of expression in this video we  will discuss further downstream analysis  steps of working with our table after  the RNA seek analysis pipeline finished  we got several files in the download  folder which were organized by type of  expression being axon Aiza form and gene  and normalization which was TPM fpkm or  raw count we can open these tables of  expression naming table manipulation  software such as Microsoft Excel  OpenOffice or even our when we started  the analysis we had a library of  unstructured data unorganized reads in a  text file also called fast queue or fast  a the output from the pipeline is called  structured data where columns are sample  names and rows our gene or isoform IDs  in in Samba format inside each cell we  will find the quantified expression  levels depending on the input and  quantification approach we selected this  number is reported in fpkm rpkm  TPM or raw count units looking through  the table you will notice that some  genes start with the letters EMS G or en  St and some start with EMS mus G or en  as mu s T these ID names show whether  these are mouse or human genes and  isoforms in some cases you will also  find road names starting with Excel OC a  special name given by cufflinks two  unidentified isoforms the Y access on  this bar chart shows the level of  expression where the bars represent an  expression profile of a given gene or  isoform this table contains thousands of  genes and isoforms so we need to  summarize this data to explore the  expression profiles of interest  one of the main challenges in downstream  analysis is to find a specific gene or  gene sets' and annotate them by their  biological functions to understand what  expression differences can be observed  and collected samples before we start  looking for specific genes we need to  summarize the full dataset to do so we  often use a box plot that shows how all  the values are distributed between  samples if we have multiple datasets  which in our case is the sample data of  gene expression with multiple samples  box plots help compare the samples using  statistical properties these are the  mean median and the bulk of the data  which is the interquartile range versus  the outliers when we prepare a box plot  of a typical RNA seek data set we can  see that the gene expression levels are  heavily skewed in linear scale in this  plot you can see that the majority of  the data points that are supposed to be  in the interquartile range are barely  visible however the outliers which  represent the top 25% of the data in the  box plot are sketched out and are  clearly visible this type of  distribution will interfere with many of  the statistical tests we plan to use to  analyze our gene expression data if we  transform our values into log scale we  will see a much more normal bar plot now  it is easier to compare values between  samples without losing the detail of  lower expressed values and still see the  highest and lowest values clearly let's  see how this transformation effects  distribution of levels in one of the  samples we are looking at here for this  sample you can see a more normal  distribution after we apply the  logarithmic scale to all the values this  procedure of normalization is often  called log normal transformation and it  makes the data more symmetrical as a  result a parametric statistical test  will provide us with more power which is  a more accurate and relevant answer  another useful transformation you might  want to consider for NGS or microarray  data is quantile normalization this is  most useful when you have a number of  conditions you want to compare  assuming the samples in each group are  biological replicates quanto  normalization arranges expression values  in similar order between samples and  aligns their means you might want to do  this for only a portion of the data for  example leaving the top express genes  untouched as a result the data will be  very close to normal distribution then  the means of each sample will be  normalized as a result some genes that  do not have very strong differences in  expression will be less significantly  different but the overall differences  between samples will not change this is  important for noisy data where thousands  of observations about each condition can  be influenced by various technical  variables to test this assumption we can  compare a couple of samples in the same  group under the same condition as TN  breast cancer and plot all the gene  expression data in a scatter plot if the  samples were identical their levels of  expression would be aligned perfectly  along the diagonal red line if they are  correlated the values would be stretched  out along this line you can see here  that the transformed data is better  correlated between samples now that we  prepared the raw expression data let's  see what we can learn about our data set  as a whole to study how gene expression  can be used to measure variability  between samples we can use a  dimensionality reduction technique that  is often used for RNA seek data  principal component analysis or PCA this  analysis method will help us to  visualize the multi-dimensional data set  in a scatter plot and see what gene  expression patterns show about the data  but first let's talk about  dimensionality what does it mean if you  look at our table of expression  we'll see that it has thousands of rows  and several columns for samples if we  take the first two genes and eight  samples we can visualize the samples in  a two dimensional space by taking the  expression values of two genes across  eight samples genes are dimensions seen  here as the X and y axis and samples are  objects which are dots in this  two-dimensional space  to incorporate additional information  offered by other genes we can add  another gene to the space and get a  three-dimensional view of our samples  but we have thousands of genes in our  table of expression how can we visualize  variants between samples using all of  this data how can we view the samples in  a multi-dimensional space which is R  raised to the N power the answer is that  you can't because we are limited by  visualization in three dimensions but  what we can do is reduce the  multi-dimensional space into two or  three dimensions that best represents  our data this is called dimensionality  reduction when we reduce the number of  dimensions to gain insights about the  data set as a whole if we use the  approach of principal components we are  looking for such a plane which are  vectors in the multi-dimensional space  we cannot see that explained variance in  our data in the best possible way here  is data with low variance the samples  are close together and here is a sample  set with higher variance  however this variance is not well  explained by the x and y axes but you  could draw a line through the original  space that shows the maximum variance of  this data set this vector through the  multi-dimensional space that maximizes  the variance of the data is called  principal component just like with our  previous example we can add other  principal components such as PC 2 or  even PC 3 thus visualizing our data by  projecting it onto a space of two or  three axes that explain some percentage  of data variance when we select the  principal components that explain the  most variance of our data we can project  all of our samples into this space and  visualize the full data set this  technique is called principal component  analysis and it can be very helpful in  the initial stages of our analysis let's  take a look again at our table of  expression the genes or rows are  dimensions and the columns are sample  names which are objects now let us run a  pipeline that will include the steps  we've discussed so far log normal  transformation quantile normalization  and principle component analysis to  visualize our data as well as see how  different samples are based on their  gene expression on the T bio info  platform select an unsupervised analysis  section under data mining once you are  inside upload the gene expression table  and tab delimited txt file pipeline we  can build here will include quant L  normalization we will transform data to  log normal scale and select the  threshold of five as well as PCA where  we can also filter the data using  various thresholds as a result of the  principal component analysis we'll get a  table of values for each principal  component associated with each object or  sample in our dataset columns will also  contain a percentage of variance that  each component explains we can visualize  our data by assigning coordinates for  each sample based on the principal  components that best explain the  variance of our data notice that we can  see a distinction between the samples  and the triple negative and estrogen  receptor-positive samples by their  positions on the x-axis we can also  notice an outlier sample in the TN group  based on the y coordinate we can liken  PCA to a photographer trying to find the  best angle for a picture that will  capture as much detail as possible while  showing us the most information about  the group as a whole we would typically  use this method to visualize and explore  our data when using this multivariate  analysis method it is important to play  around with the data using normalization  and filtering while paying attention to  outliers for the best possible  interpretation in this video we reviewed  several approaches important for working  with our table of expression log normal  transformation quantile normalization  and principal component analysis at the  end of this course you will have a  chance to review all the contents  covered as well as practice what you've  learned on sample projects
 

